; a `def` only gives a name to its right-hand side, so it can't prove anything new: the new
; symbol applied to distinct variables on the left, no other variables on the right, not for an
; operator declared `sym` or `flat`, and not inside a proof block (found in the soundness review
; of 2026-09-29, doc/kurt-soundness.md §8.17) -- each of these used to give `1 = 2` or `false`
load numbers, logic
expect "EvalError"
    def c = $y                      ; `c = 1` and `c = 2`
expect "EvalError"
    def c * 0 = 1                   ; `0 = 1` by "mul-zero"
expect "EvalError"
    def f ($x + $y) = $x            ; `f (1 + 2)` is `1` and `2`
expect "EvalError"
    def f ($x * 0) = $x
expect "EvalError"
    def g($x, $x) = $x              ; not distinct
expect "EvalError"
    def P iff %A                    ; `P` and `not P`

; `sym`/`flat` are claims about an operator, which a `def` can't keep
infix ## 60 60
sym ##
expect "EvalError"
    def $a ## $b = $a - $b          ; `1 - 2 = 2 - 1`
infix #& 60 60
flat #&
expect "EvalError"
    def $a #& $b = $a - $b

; a new symbol must head the defined expression, not be an argument of an existing operator
expect "EvalError"
    def $x * k = $x                 ; `0 * k` is `0` by "mul-zero", so `0 = $x` for every `$x`
infix is 30 30
const is
expect "EvalError"
    def $f is injective iff forall $x forall $y ($f $x = $f $y ⇒ $x = $y)
def injective($f) iff forall $x forall $y ($f $x = $f $y ⇒ $x = $y)

; what a `def` is for still works
def h($x) = $x * $x
infix ∘ 40 40
def ($f ∘ $g) $x = $f ($g $x)       ; curried: `∘` applied to `$f`, `$g`, and the result to `$x`
calc on
h(3) = 9
